ion to assume this  unwanted posture is a violence that's  it's expected to do to itself and this  is something that students experience  when they're trying to learn some new  disciplinary knowledge think about  trying to learn a language and trying to  wrap your tongue around it and then  trying to memorize this stuff and I know  there's other ways to learn languages as  well but just go with me on this so  you're trying to wrap your head around  these these foreign you know ways of  thinking about things declining nouns  and conjugating verbs and talking about  the subjunctive or you know depending on  what language it is adapting yourself to  that feels kind of painful at first it  feels unnatural but that's part of  what's required on the other hand if  disciplinary knowledge is to really be  what it wants to be it has to take into  account the individual  it has to become more flexible than it  is this is again why Hegelian science  would be something quite different than  what we often term science so he says  let science be in its own self what it  may relative to immediate  self-consciousness it prevents that it  presents itself in an inverted posture  or because this self-consciousness has  the principal of its actual existence in  the certainty of itself science appears  to it not to be actual and again a  common thing you've got your book  learning but I've got actual experience  because science doesn't experience  individuals experience science can only  be derived and develop through actually  having individual people who become  scientists so this leads us to where we  really want to go we have the moment of  you know clash and under familiarity so  he says science must therefore unite  this element to self certainty with  itself or rather show that and how this  element belongs to it implicitly it's  already there so long as science lacks  this actual dimension it is only the  content as the in itself it is the  objective but that's it it may have  p